7621426657e-05,-0.6672000288963318,-0.0002324311062693596,-5.960462772236497e-07,-0.00019751029321923852,0.0,-0.0007595514762215316,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,-0.002597531769424677,-1.5497195136049413e-06,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-0.002921363105997443,0.0,-2.622600959512056e-06,-4.0531076592742465e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,785,47311,400,79,2075,15087,67901,400,79,7,16,8,284,220,17,16,15,3,323,198,59,9697,87,488,220,16,15,8,281,7,17,87,8,284,220,23,2075,481,220,19,8,281,2075,488,220,21,10699,60,1958,678,1931,5109,400,87,2418,220,7379,400,79,7,16,15,568,3,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe polynomial $p(x)$ satisfies $p(1) = 210$ and\n\\[(x + 10) p(2x) = 8(x - 4) p(x + 6)\\]for all real numbers $x.$  Find $p(10).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"662d156f48ca7b07de17476e2f9ad4da082b76e5839bea23146cb98f6a33c1ee"},"seconds":70.55030584335327,"seed":362912942825928943,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for \\( p(10) \\), we start by analyzing the given functional equation:\n\\[\n(x + 10) p(2x) = 8(x - 4) p(x + 6).\n\\]\n\nWe are given \\( p(1) = 210 \\). Let's substitute \\( x = -10 \\) into the equation to explore the behavior of \\( p(x) \\):\n\\[\n(-10 + 10) p(2(-10)) = 8(-10 - 4) p(-10 + 6).\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n0 \\cdot p(-20) = 8(-14) p(-4).\n\\]\nSince \\( 0 \\cdot p(-20) = 0 \\), we get:\n\\[\n0 = -112 p(-4).\n\\]\nThus, \\( p(-4) = 0 \\).\n\nNext, let's substitute \\( x = 4 \\) into the original equation:\n\\[\n(4 + 10) p(2 \\cdot 4) = 8(4 - 4) p(4 + 6).\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n14 p(8) = 8 \\cdot 0 \\cdot p(10).\n\\]\nSince \\( 8 \\cdot 0 \\cdot p(10) = 0 \\), we get:\n\\[\n14 p(8) = 0.\n\\]\nThus, \\( p(8) = 0 \\).\n\nNow, we have found that \\( p(-4) = 0 \\) and \\( p(8) = 0 \\). This suggests that \\( p(x) \\) has roots at \\( x = -4 \\) and \\( x = 8 \\). We can express \\( p(x) \\) in the form:\n\\[\np(x) = c(x + 4)(x - 8)q(x),\n\\]\nwhere \\( q(x) \\) is another polynomial and \\( c \\) is a constant.\n\nTo find \\( c \\) and \\( q(x) \\), we use the given \\( p(1) = 210 \\):\n\\[\np(1) = c(1 + 4)(1 - 8)q(1) = 210.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nc \\cdot 5 \\cdot (-7) \\cdot q(1) = 210,\n\\]\n\\[\n-35c q(1) = 210,\n\\]\n\\[\nc q(1) = -6.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine \\( p(10) \\):\n\\[\np(10) = c(10 + 4)(10 - 8)q(10) = c \\cdot 14 \\cdot 2 \\cdot q(10) = 28c q(10).\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( q(10) \\), we substitute \\( x = 10 \\) into the original equation:\n\\[\n(10 + 10) p(2 \\cdot 10) = 8(10 - 4) p(10 + 6).\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n20 p(20) = 48 p(16).\n\\]\n\nUsing \\( p(x) = c(x + 4)(x - 8)q(x) \\):\n\\[\np(20) = c(20 + 4)(20 - 8)q(20) = c \\cdot 24 \\cdot 12 \\cdot q(20) = 288c q(20),\n\\]\n\\[\np(16) = c(16 + 4)(16 - 8)q(16) = c \\cdot 20 \\cdot 8 \\cdot q(16) = 160c q(16).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these into the equation:\n\\[\n20 \\cdot 288c q(20) = 48 \\cdot 160c q(16),\n\\]\n\\[\n5760c q(20) = 7680c q(16),\n\\]\n\\[\n3q(20) = 4q(16).\n\\]\n\nSince \\( q(x) \\) must be consistent with this relationship, we assume \\( q(x) \\) is a constant polynomial, say \\( q(x) = k \\). Then:\n\\[\np(x) = c(x + 4)(x - 8)k,\n\\]\n\\[\np(1) = c \\cdot 5 \\cdot (-7) \\cdot k = 210,\n\\]\n\\[\n-35ck = 210,\n\\]\n\\[\nck = -6.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\\[\np(10) = 28c q(10) = 28c k = 28 \\cdot (-6) = -168.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-168}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{-168}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
